1\tSpeechAgent-R (ours)\n542\t96.74\n543\t78.59\n544\t84.17\n545\t20.85\n546\t79.61\n547\t65.80\n548\t70.94\n549\t25.45\n550\t80.05\n551\t22.29\n552\tTable 1: \n553\tMain results on HIU-Bench, reported separately on ID\n554\tand OOD splits.\n555\tOverall summarizes performance across both splits and all Time values are reported in seconds.\n556\tBoldface and underlining denote the best and\n557\tsecond-best results in each column, respectively.\n558\tGray shading highlights our models.\n559\tSetting\n560\tIn-domain (ID)\n561\tOut-of-domain (OOD)\n562\tOverall\n563\tTool\n564\tAnswer\n565\tTotal\n566\tTool\n567\tAnswer\n568\tTotal\n569\tBase\n570\t–\n571\t–\n572\t40.67\n573\t–\n574\t–\n575\t45.25\n576\t42.10\n577\t+ Agent harness\n578\t78.98\n579\t63.53\n580\t68.77\n581\t65.56\n582\t51.12\n583\t56.71\n584\t65.01\n585\t+ SFT (answer-only)\n586\t71.25\n587\t67.26\n588\t69.89\n589\t58.85\n590\t56.52\n591\t59.22\n592\t66.57\n593\t+ SFT\n594\t97.94\n595\t70.74\n596\t78.92\n597\t78.51\n598\t60.56\n599\t66.98\n600\t75.20\n601\t\\rowcolorgray!15\n602\t+ SFT + RL (Ours)\n603\t96.74\n604\t78.59\n605\t84.17\n606\t79.61\n607\t65.80\n608\t70.94\n609\t80.05\n610\tTable 2: Ablation of the agent framework and training strategies on\n611\tHIU-Bench. Best and second-best results are shown in bold and underlined,\n612\trespectively, gray denotes our final model.\n613\t5.1 Benchmark Construction and Statistics\n614\tHIU-Bench comprises an ID split that follows the same task, skill and tool distributions as HIU-Corpus while using independent audio instances, and an OOD split constructed through the same pipeline with new task scenarios and skill compositions.\n615\tIt contains 1,395 samples across 56 tasks, including 960 samples from 24 ID tasks and 435 samples from 32 OOD tasks.\n616\tWe check data independence between HIU-Corpus and HIU-Bench at the audio and task levels.\n617\tFile-level matching and MD5 content verification find no binary-identical audio overlap in model inputs, while OOD tasks are disjoint from HIU-Corpus at the task level.\n618\tSpeaker overlap in ID follows the splits of the source datasets, with no additional speaker leakage introduced during benchmark construction.\n619\tFurthermore, we compare tool coverage and workflow composition between ID and OOD.\n620\tFig. 4(a) shows different tool-family coverage, with OOD introducing additional tool categories, while Fig. 4(b) shows 12 and 21 unique gold tool chains with no overlap.\n621\tThese differences enable HIU-Bench to evaluate tool coordination across diverse task scenarios and workflow compositions.\n622\t5.2 Evaluation Protocol\n623\tWe define StotalS_{\\mathrm{total}} on HIU-Bench from three evaluation dimensions: format correctness (SfS_{f}), tool interaction (StS_{t}) and answer quality (SaS_{a}), weighted by 0.05/0.25/0.70, with their effects analyzed in Section 6.3.\n624\tFor direct-answer evaluation, only SaS_{a} is used.\n625\tFor agent evaluation, SfS_{f} and SaS_{a} follow the same criteria as RfR_{f} and RaR_{a}, while StS_{t} is evaluated separately for ID and OOD tasks.\n626\tFor ID tasks, StS_{t} follows the training-time scoring function, whereas OOD tasks adopt a workflow-aware rubric: expected tool usage receives full scores, valid alternatives partial scores, and invalid calls or failed executions limited scores.\n627\tOverall performance is computed as the sample-weighted average of ID and OOD scores.\n628\tThe evaluation metrics follow the same task definitions as training rewards but are independently computed on held-out annotations, preventing direct reuse of training signals.\n629\t6 Experiments\n630\tTool\n631\tMetric\n632\tBase\n633\tSFT\n634\tOurs\n635\tSpeaker diarization\n636\t100⋅(1−DER)100\\cdot(1-\\mathrm{DER})\n637\t75.7\n638\t76.6\n639\t77.4\n640\tSpeaker verification\n641\tVerified acc.\n642\t84.3\n643\t89.5\n644\t88.7\n645\tAnti-spoof detection\n646\tLabel acc.\n647\t52.5\n648\t71.2\n649\t71.2\n650\tSound event detector\n651\tTop-5 acc.\n652\t100.0\n653\t86.8\n654\t87.2\n655\tSpeech DOA estimator\n656\tAcc.@10∘10^{\\circ}\n657\t100.0\n658\t100.0\n659\t100.0\n660\tTarget-speaker extraction\n661\tSI-SDRi\n662\t61.9\n663\t62.2\n664\t62.2\n665\tSpatial target-speech extraction\n666\tSI-SDRi\n667\t26.9\n668\t32.0\n669\t41.6\n670\tSpeech separation\n671\tSI-SDRi\n672\t74.5\n673\t77.4\n674\t75.9\n675\t\\rowcolor\n676\tgray!15\n677\tOverall\n678\t72.0\n679\t74.5\n680\t75.5\n681\tTable 3: \n682\tPer-tool performance on the ID split of HIU-Bench.\n683\tEach tool is evaluated using the metric specified in the second column,\n684\tand Overall denotes the macro-average across all tools.\n685\tHigher values indicate better performance for all metrics.\n686\tFigure 4: Comparison of tool coverage and workflow compositions between ID and OOD splits in HIU-Bench. (a) Tool-family distribution. (b) Gold workflow compositions and their overlap across splits.\n687\t6.1 Experimental Setup\n688\tSpeechAgent-R is initialized from Qwen3-Omni-Thinking and trained on 24 NVIDIA A100 GPUs using ms-swift in BF16.\n689\tSFT fully fine-tunes the Thinker with a learning rate of 1×10−61\\times 10^{-6} and a global batch size of 32.\n690\tFor GRPO, we apply LoRA to all Thinker linear layers with rank 16 and alpha 32.\n691\tThe training uses a learning rate of 5×10−75\\times 10^{-7}, a group size of 4, ϵ=0.2\\epsilon=0.2, β=0.04\\beta=0.04, and reward weights (0.05,0.25,0.70)(0.05,0.25,0.70) for 150 steps.\n692\tThe step-80 checkpoint is selected based on validation performance, and all models are evaluated under the same inference budget and agent environment.\n693\tWe evaluate SpeechAgent-R on HIU-Bench against Kimi-Audio (KimiTeam, Ding, and Ju 2025), Step-Audio-R1 (Tian, Zhang, and Zhang 2025), MiDashengLM (Dinkel, Li, and Liu 2025), Qwen2-Audio (Chu, Xu, and Yang 2024), Qwen2.5-Omni (Team 2026), Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview and Gemini-3-Flash-Preview in direct and agent settings using the same scoring protocol.\n694\tFigure 5: Representative cases comparing the trajectories generated by the SFT and RL models, illustrating differences in skill selection, tool usage and response generation.\n695\t6.2 Main Results on HIU-Bench\n696\tTable 1 presents the results under two settings.\n697\tUnder the direct setting, all models achieve limited performance, with overall scores ranging from 33.90 to 45.89.\n698\tThese limitations are particularly evident in complex audio scenarios, such as spatial audio understanding and overlapping-audio analysis, where models must extract and integrate task-relevant acoustic evidence.\n699\tThe shared agent harness improves most models, with Step-Audio-R1 rising from 33.90 to 46.24 and Gemini-3-Flash-Preview from 42.22 to 68.08.\n700\tHowever, the improvements vary across models: access to the same skills and tools does not lead to effective tool use by itself.\n701\tFor example, Kimi-Audio achieves only 26.60 overall score, as unsuccessful tool interactions and format violations offset the benefits of tool access.\n702\tMoreover, some models also incur additional inference latency due to longer interaction trajectories.\n703\tThese results highlight the need for training audio agents that can effectively utilize skills and tools and generalize such capabilities to unseen scenarios.\n704\tAfter SFT on verified interaction trajectories from HIU-Corpus, SpeechAgent-SFT improves the overall score from 65.01 to 75.20 under the same agent harness, with gains on ID and OOD splits.\n705\tHowever, OOD analysis shows that SFT still faces tool-use challenges: 32.4% of failures come from incorrect tool selection or poor tool-result use, and 18.2% from ineffective interaction strategies.\n706\tWith GRPO optimization, SpeechAgent-R achieves the highest overall score of 80.05, outperforming the base model with agent harness and SpeechAgent-SFT by 15.04 and 4.85 points.\n707\tOOD error analysis shows that GRPO improves agent behavior beyond supervised trajectories: compared with SFT, SpeechAgent-R reduces incorrect tool selection failures by 32.8% and tool-result utilization failures by 16.3%, enabling more effective skill use and response generation.\n708\t6.3 Experiments Analysis\n709\tAblation studies of different training strategies\n710\tTable 2 analyzes the effects of training strategies.\n711\tAnswer-only SFT, which supervises only final responses, achieves 66.57 overall, providing only a small improvement over the agent baseline, whereas trajectory-based SFT reaches 75.20 with gains on both ID and OOD tasks.\n712\tBuilding on SFT, GRPO further improves the score by 4.85 points to 80.05, showing that interaction trajectories establish agent behaviors and GRPO further refines skill selection and tool use.\n713\tReward weight ablations show that 0.05/0.25/0.70 weighting achieves the best overall score, while higher answer weights degrade tool-use performance.\n714\tIncreasing the answer weight to 0.8 achieves 79.99 but causes more tool-use failures, highlighting the need to balance task performance and interaction reliability.\n715\tTool Interaction Analysis\n716\tTable 3 reports tool-level performance on the ID test set.\n717\tAcross different training strategies, tool execution performance remains comparable, with average scores of 72.0, 74.5, and 75.5 for Base, SFT, and SpeechAgent-R.\n718\tThese results suggest that the gains mainly come from improved agent-side skill and tool utilization rather than changes in tool capabilities.\n719\tTo investigate the remaining performance bottleneck of SpeechAgent-R, we evaluate its upper bound under an oracle tool setting, where ground-truth tool outputs replace actual tool responses to eliminate tool execution errors.\n720\tAs shown in Table 4, the overall score improves from 84.2 to 91.4, mainly driven by the increase in Answer score from 78.6 to 88.5, while Tool-call remains nearly unchanged.\n721\tThese results show that, after reliable skill selection and tool invocation are achieved, further improvements mainly depend on tool output quality and the model’s multimodal understanding and reasoning ability.\n722\tFurther Analysis\n723\tComponent\n724\tNormal\n725\tOracle\n726\tΔ\\Delta\n727\tFormat\n728\t99.5\n729\t99.3\n730\t-0.2\n731\tTool-call\n732\t96.7\n733\t97.9\n734\t+1.2\n735\tAnswer\n736\t78.6\n737\t88.5\n738\t+9.9\n739\t\\rowcolor\n740\tgray!15\n741\tTotal\n742\t84.2\n743\t91.4\n744\t+7.2\n745\tTable 4: Oracle-tool analysis on the HIU-Bench ID split. Δ\\Delta denotes Oracle minus Normal.\n746\tFigure 5 presents three representative cases showing how SpeechAgent-R improves tool interaction beyond SFT.\n747\tIn Case 1, SpeechAgent-R improves tool-result utilization by integrating tool outputs with its own audio understanding, using multimodal evidence to refine predictions when tool responses are incomplete.\n748\tIn Case 2, SpeechAgent-R better understands task requirements and selects the appropriate skill, while SFT initially invokes an irrelevant skill and requires additional exploration.\n749\tIn Case 3, SpeechAgent-R further demonstrates skill-guided tool execution by understanding the relationship between skills and executable tools.\n750\tIt follows the skill requirements to invoke the necessary tools for the evaluation workflow, whereas SFT directly treats the skill name as an executable tool and produces an invalid tool call.\n751\tTogether, these cases show that SpeechAgent-R learns a reliable interaction process, covering skill selection, tool execution, and tool-result reasoning, which enables better generalization to complex audio tasks.\n752\t7 Conclusion\n753\tIn this paper, we present SpeechAgent-R, a skill-calling multimodal agent that enables audio models to handle complex acoustic tasks through adaptive interaction.\n754\tThrough trajectory-based SFT on large-scale interaction data from HIU-Corpus and subsequent multi-turn RL, SpeechAgent-R learns to select skills, coordinate tools and use tool feedback for subsequent decisions beyond fixed-input audio understanding.\n755\tWe further introduce HIU-Bench to evaluate audio agents under both in-distribution and out-of-distribution settings.\n756\tSpeechAgent-R achieves an overall score of 80.05 on HIU-Bench, improving over its base model under the same agent harness by 15.40 points and demonstrating improved skill and tool coordination across diverse task and workflow configurations.\n757\tOne limitation is that, although HIU-Bench covers diverse task settings, extending it to more complex and realistic audio agent scenarios remains an important direction for future work.\n758\tWe will release the code, models, and datasets upon publication to support future research.\n759\tReferences\n760\tBu, Du, and Na (2017)\n761\tBu, H.; Du, J.; and Na, X. 2017.\n762\tAISHELL-1: An Open-Source Mandarin Speech Corpus and A Speech Recognition Baseline.\n763\tCoRR, abs/1709.05522.\n764\tChen, Chen, and Cai (2026)\n765\tChen, L.; Chen, H.; and Cai, Y. 2026.\n766\tAudioRouter: Data Efficient Audio Understanding via RL based Dual Reasoning.\n767\tCoRR, abs/2602.10439.\n768\tChu, Xu, and Yang (2024)\n769\tChu, Y.; Xu, J.; and Yang, Q. 2024.\n770\tQwen2-Audio Technical Report.\n771\tCoRR, abs/2407.10759.\n772\tCosentino et al. (2020)\n773\tCosentino, J.; Pariente, M.; Cornell, S.; Deleforge, A.; and Vincent, E. 2020.\n774\tLibriMix: An Open-Source Dataset for Generalizable Speech Separation.\n775\tarXiv:2005.11262.\n776\tDinkel, Li, and Liu (2025)\n777\tDinkel, H.; Li, G.; and Liu, J. 2025.\n778\tMiDashengLM: Efficient Audio Understanding with General Audio Captions.\n779\tCoRR, abs/2508.03983.\n780\tFonseca, Favory, and Pons (2020)\n781\tFonseca, E.; Favory, X.; and Pons, J. 2020.\n782\tFSD50K: an Open Dataset of Human-Labeled Sound Events.\n783\tCoRR, abs/2010.00475.\n784\tFu, Cheng, and Lv (2021)\n785\tFu, Y.; Cheng, L.; and Lv, S. 2021.\n786\tAISHELL-4: An Open Source Dataset for Speech Enhancement, Separation, Recognition and Speaker Diarization in Conferenc